    WHY NOT THE EDUCATIONAL TIER — even though the authors would qualify for it under
    §1.2.4 ("universities, schools, colleges, academies ... by students and teachers in
    structured educational programs"). Three reasons, and the first is decisive:

      1. IT IS NOT PERPETUAL. §1.2.4, last sentence: "Notwithstanding any reference to a
         perpetual licence, an Educational Licence shall only come into effect and endure
         for the period of time the Education Licence requirements are met." The licence
         therefore ENDS when the student graduates or the teacher leaves — and with it the
         §1.9 right to keep developing and Distributing. Every installer already published
         would be sitting on GitHub with no licence behind it. Starter is perpetual, so a
         published release stays covered forever.
      2. IT BARS "PROMOTIONAL" USE. §1.2.4: "may not be used for any commercial,
         professional, promotional, or any other for-profit activity." Exhibitions, open
         days, showreels and any event an organisation uses to promote itself all read as
         promotional, and running the show software at one would be arguable. Starter has
         no such clause — it cares only about revenue, and a free exhibition earns none.
      3. IT CARRIES A PROOF OBLIGATION. §1.2.4: licensees "must be able to provide RMS
         with proof of their status ... upon request", which ties the project's compliance
         to paperwork about two people's enrolment/employment rather than to the project.

    Educational stays available to any institution that wants to teach with JUCE — free,
    uncapped, and precisely its purpose. That is the institution's own election, unrelated
    to this project, and it would not replace the authors' seats (§1.7) in any case.

    ── THE STARTER REVENUE TEST, IN THE EULA'S OWN TERMS (read it yourself: §1.2.1) ──
    Starter is FREE and PERPETUAL ("Perpetual Price per user: Free"), capped at "Up to
    $20,000" annual revenue. What counts as revenue depends on WHO the licensee is, and
    the two definitions are not the same:

      · AN INDIVIDUAL (or an entity with no separate legal identity) counts "the total
        revenue or funding generated by that individual or entity's USE OF THE FRAMEWORK
        from all sources, including donations, sponsorship, advertising, and any other
        indirect revenue from their use of the Framework".
        → Scoped to JUCE-related income. ArtLux is given away under a licence that forbids
          charging for it (LICENSE §2) and takes no donations or sponsorship, so this
          figure is ZERO, and zero is comfortably inside $20,000.

      · A COMPANY counts "the total revenue or funding received by the entity and all its
        Affiliates ... from all sources, WHETHER IT BE RECEIVED IN CONNECTION WITH THE
        ENTITY'S USE OF THE FRAMEWORK OR NOT, without offsets of any kind".
        → NOT scoped to JUCE. If a company is the licensee, every euro that company and
          its affiliates earn from ANY activity counts against the $20,000 — unrelated
          client work included. A studio doing commercial work on the side would blow the
          cap while ArtLux itself earned nothing.

    SO THE LICENSEE MUST BE THE TWO INDIVIDUALS — never a company, studio or institution.
    This is the one thing that could quietly void the election: any such entity's revenue
    counts in full, "whether it be received in connection with the entity's use of the
    Framework or not", so an organisation earning anything at all from unrelated activity
    would be past the $20,000 while ArtLux itself earned nothing. Keeping the seats personal
    keeps the test at zero. It is also why the copyright line names the two authors and no
    organisation; see (f) below for the two remaining company-shaped identifiers and why
    they are not authorship claims.

    ALSO: §1.7 — "Every Framework User contributing to or modifying source code ...
    requires a Licence." Two people work on the audio engine, so that is TWO Starter
    seats, one each, registered individually. Free, but not automatic.

    NOT A TRAP HERE: §1.10's "keep all seats active to continue Distributing" applies to
    SUBSCRIPTION licences. Starter is perpetual, so there is no renewal that could
    retroactively strand a published release.

    IF THE CAP IS EVER EXCEEDED: §1.14 — "you must either purchase a new Licence Type
    appropriate for your new Revenue or Funding Limit or immediately cease developing and
    Distributing Products." Take the GitHub releases down or upgrade the tier; there is no
    grace period in the text.

